= Cache memory — Simple =
<onlyinclude>A '''cache''' is a small, fast place where a computer keeps copies of information it expects to use again. When the information is already there, the processor can often retrieve it sooner than by asking a more distant memory level.</onlyinclude>

== An everyday analogy ==
Imagine keeping frequently used reference books on your desk instead of returning to a library shelf each time. A desk is faster to reach but holds fewer books. Choosing which books stay on the desk resembles a cache's replacement policy. Unlike a desk, a computer cache also tracks precisely which memory addresses its copies represent.

== What happens on a request? ==
# The processor requests an address.
# Cache hardware checks a location determined by the address and compares stored tags.
# If a matching valid line is present, the request is a '''hit'''.
# Otherwise it is a '''miss'''; the computer obtains the line from another level and may evict an older line.

== Why does organization matter? ==
Some addresses compete for the same limited slots even when other slots are empty. A set-associative cache offers several slots per set. A write-back cache may hold modified data that has not yet been written to the next level. Multiple processors may need a coherence protocol to avoid using stale copies of shared writable data.

== What a cache cannot promise ==
A cache does not make every access faster: misses cost time, and extra cache circuitry consumes energy. Actual performance depends on the program's access pattern and the processor design.

